        "header": "3VibeVideoBench",
        "images": [
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.35864/2610.35864v2/overview_art.png",
                "caption": "Figure 2:Overview of VibeVideoBench.(a)Tasks seeded from four kinds of real-world sources are drafted under a shared contract, given the assets they need (searched for or synthesized by an LLM), and curated by humans, yielding a benchmark that is diverse in content, form, length and sound.(b)Each task gets a three-level rubric traced to its prompt and its input assets; L2 checks quote their basis and carry modality tags; rubrics go through rounds of model review and human review until no flag holds.(c)Agents run in native harnesses in identical one-hour sandboxes; programs check L1, and three VLM judge panels—one routing each check by modality—score L2 and L3.",
                "position": 558
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.35864/2610.35864v2/taxonomy_ring.png",
                "caption": "Figure 3:Benchmark composition (239 tasks).Top:the inner ring gives the 12 categories, with spans proportional to task counts. The outer ring names 24 of the 34 presentation forms, each shown once, in the category where it is most characteristic (equal angles, not counts); every task can carry several of the 43 subcategory tags, and the 9 knowledge domains are not drawn. The six cards are real deliveries, one per model family.Bottom:(a) prompt length of the 209 English tasks (lessons and papers keep their content in the supplied note or PDF, so their prompts are short); (b) target runtime, 8 s–15 min; (c) L2 checks per task, 13–110; (d) share of tasks with each requirement.",
                "position": 574
            }
        ]
